  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nShowers associated with a shortwave trough and weak sfc low tracking \nacross Pennsylvania will start to push into NE PA during the mid-\nmorning hours today. Showers will push further north into the rest \nof the forecast area by the early afternoon hours as a warm front \ntracks northeast. Showers will continue through tonight and into \nMonday as another shortwave trough crosses the region during the \nevening, followed by a weak cold front that will slowly track across \nthe area on Monday. An influx of moisture increasing PWat values up \nto around 2.00 inches will provide the potential for heavier \nshowers. The heavier showers with the rain should stay mostly in PA \ncloser to the stronger portion of the two shortwave troughs. Showers \nare expected to come to an end during the first half of the \novernight on Monday night. Forecast guidance continues with the \nlower rainfall amounts, with amounts of around a tenth to quarter of \nan inch is expected for NY zones, with amounts increasing to the \nsouth toward three quarters of an inch in the southern portions of \nthe forecast area, including from Luzerne County to Pike County. \nThunderstorms should be limited through this evening with limited \ninstability. \n\nWinds will increase over the area as the pressure gradient increases \nover the region. Wind gusts in the 20 to 25 mph range will be \npossible, especially over the higher terrain. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front associated with a sfc low near Hudson Bay will cross \nthe region on Wednesday evening. Ahead of the frontal boundary, \nshowers will move into the region late Wednesday morning and \nafternoon, continuing into the evening on Wednesday. Afternoon \ninstability with daytime heating ahead of the front will allow for \nsome thunderstorms to develop on Wednesday afternoon as well. \nUncertainty still exists among guidance for how quickly the front \nwill track through the region, as some guidance still tries to bring \na sfc low northeast along the frontal boundary. This scenario will \nboth increase rainfall and duration of showers expected into \nThursday. Most guidance is fairly quick with passing front \notherwise.",
